---
import CourseLayout from "../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const examples = home + "examples/tcp/";
const createFile =
  'python3 -c \'from pathlib import Path; Path("hello.txt").write_bytes(b"HELLO")\'';
const dockerServer =
  "docker run --rm --name tcp-demo-server --network tcp-demo \\\n" +
  '  --mount "type=bind,src=$PWD,dst=/lesson" -w /lesson python:3.12-slim \\\n' +
  "  python -u server.py --host 0.0.0.0";
const dockerClient =
  "docker run --rm --network tcp-demo \\\n" +
  '  --mount "type=bind,src=$PWD,dst=/lesson,readonly" -w /lesson python:3.12-slim \\\n' +
  "  python client.py hello.txt --host tcp-demo-server";
---

<CourseLayout
  page="tcp-demo"
  title="TCP demo tutorials | Computer Networks"
  description="Run a two-minute TCP file-transfer demo. Try the downloadable Python examples on one laptop, a phone hotspot, or Docker."
>
  <div class="intro lab-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Demo tutorials</p>
    <h1>
      Send a file over <span>TCP</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Send a five-byte file, check the reply, and practice explaining what
      happened. Start with two terminals, then try two laptops on a phone
      hotspot or two Docker containers.
    </p>
    <div class="intro-links">
      <a class="button primary" href="#loopback">
        Start with one laptop
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={examples + "server.py"} download>
        Download server.py
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={examples + "client.py"} download>
        Download client.py
      </a>
    </div>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <a href="#loopback">Rehearse a two-minute transfer</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#hotspot-docker">Connect over a hotspot or Docker</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#client-dashboard">Plan a client and dashboard</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#private-server">Try a private remote server</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="loopback">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="loopback" tabindex="-1">
        1. Rehearse on one laptop
      </h2>
      <p>
        Save the two scripts in a fresh folder and open two terminals in that
        folder. You need Python 3. The server defaults to loopback (
        <code>127.0.0.1</code>), so the demonstration works with Wi-Fi off.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Server terminal. Start listening.</strong>
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>python3 server.py</code></pre>
        <p>
          Wait for <code>Waiting on 127.0.0.1:5001</code>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Client terminal. Create and send the sample.</strong>
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>{createFile + "\npython3 client.py hello.txt"}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The client prints <code>OK. The server saved 5 bytes.</code> The
          received copy is <code>received-file.bin</code>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the copy and reset.</strong>
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>shasum -a 256 hello.txt received-file.bin</code></pre>
        <p>
          On macOS, the two hashes should match. On Linux you can also use
          <code>sha256sum</code>. To repeat, rename the received copy before
          starting the server again. The server handles one transfer per run and
          protects existing files from being overwritten.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Two-minute run of show</h3>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>0:00–0:25.</strong> Show the waiting server and name the IP and
        port.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>0:25–0:55.</strong> Run the client. Explain that it sends a
        40-byte header with the file size and SHA-256 fingerprint, followed by
        the file. TCP delivers an ordered stream of bytes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>0:55–1:30.</strong> Point to the saved file and the OK line. The
        server checks the received fingerprint, saves the file, and returns a
        one-byte application receipt.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>1:30–2:00.</strong> Show the matching hashes if time allows.
        Explain that the fingerprint checks the contents even when two files
        have the same size.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="note">
      <h3>Prepare the fallback before you present</h3>
      <p>
        Rehearse twice on the presentation laptop with Wi-Fi off. Enlarge both
        terminals, pre-type the client command, and save a local recording or
        screenshots from a successful rehearsal. If an error appears or five
        seconds pass without OK, switch to the recording and keep explaining.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="hotspot-docker">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="hotspot-docker" tabindex="-1">
        2. Try a hotspot or Docker
      </h2>
      <p>
        Use the same scripts and show how the client finds a different machine.
      </p>
    </div>
    <h3>Two laptops on one phone hotspot</h3>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        Join both laptops to the <strong>same phone hotspot</strong>. Save the
        scripts on their respective laptops before the rehearsal. Use a
        synthetic practice file such as HELLO.
      </li>
      <li>
        Find the server <em>laptop’s</em> IPv4 address in its Wi-Fi settings,
        then replace the placeholder below in both commands. Use the laptop’s
        address on the hotspot rather than the phone’s gateway address.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the server laptop, allow the connection on this network if the local
        firewall prompts you. Start the server and wait for its waiting message.
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>python3 server.py --host SERVER_LAPTOP_IP</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        On the client laptop, create the sample and send it.
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>{createFile + "\npython3 client.py hello.txt --host SERVER_LAPTOP_IP"}</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Rehearse with the actual phone and laptops. The file crosses your local
      hotspot network. If the laptops cannot reach each other, recheck the
      server’s assigned IP and use the one-laptop demo if needed.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Two containers on one laptop</summary>
      <p>
        In the folder with both scripts, pull the image before the demo. This
        exercise creates a dedicated Docker network. Rename any existing
        <code>received-file.bin</code> in the folder before starting.
      </p>
      <pre class="lab-code"><code>{createFile + "\ndocker pull python:3.12-slim\ndocker network create tcp-demo"}</code></pre>
      <p>Start the server container in one terminal.</p>
      <pre
        class="lab-code"
        tabindex="0"
        aria-label="Docker server command"
      ><code>{dockerServer}</code></pre>
      <p>
        In the second terminal, send the file to the server container by name.
      </p>
      <pre
        class="lab-code"
        tabindex="0"
        aria-label="Docker client command"
      ><code>{dockerClient}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The received file appears in your working folder. Both containers share
        the Docker network, so they can talk without publishing a host port. To
        reach the server from the host, publish its port to loopback with{" "}
        <code>-p 127.0.0.1:5001:5001</code> before the image name in the server
        command. The server container exits after one transfer. When finished,
        run
        <code>docker network rm tcp-demo</code>.
        <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/bridge/">
          Docker bridge networking
        </a>
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="client-dashboard">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="client-dashboard" tabindex="-1">
        3. Plan a client and dashboard
      </h2>
      <p>
        Keep the TCP transfer and improve how you start it and read the result.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Desktop client.</strong> Try{" "}
        <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/tkinter.html">Tkinter</a>
        for a file picker, a destination field, and a Send button. Run the
        teaching client in a background process and show its real success or
        error output.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local activity dashboard.</strong> Start with the server
        terminal. To build a page that updates on its own, first add structured
        events for connection, received bytes, duration, and verdict. The
        teaching server prints a waiting line and final byte count. Each
        additional metric needs to be recorded by your code.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Browser client.</strong> A normal web page uploads to a local
        HTTP bridge such as{" "}
        <a href="https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/request-files/">
          FastAPI
        </a>
        . The bridge then opens a separate raw TCP connection to the teaching
        server. Treat that integration as its own project.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Check your work.</strong> A successful send should show the same
      received byte count and verdict in the client and the server view. A
      stopped server should show a fresh error instead of the previous result.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="private-server">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="private-server" tabindex="-1">
        4. Try a private remote server
      </h2>
      <p>
        Use this extension to learn what changes when the server is on another
        network.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>Put the teaching server on a VM you control with Python 3.</li>
      <li>
        Connect the VM and your laptop to a private network such as
        <a href="https://tailscale.com/docs/features/access-control">
          Tailscale
        </a>
        . Allow the demo laptop to reach port 5001 and keep it closed on the
        public interface.
      </li>
      <li>
        Start the server with{" "}
        <code>python3 server.py --host VM_PRIVATE_IP</code>. On your laptop,
        send HELLO with
        <code>python3 client.py hello.txt --host VM_PRIVATE_IP</code>.
        Substitute the VM’s private address in both commands.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check the application’s OK reply and compare the file hashes on the two
        machines. Stop the listener after the rehearsal.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="lesson-next">
      <div>
        <h3>See what TCP sends</h3>
        <p>
          Use a separate sample trace to find the handshake, data, and reply.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="button primary" href={home + "labs/wireshark/"}>
        Open the Wireshark lab
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</CourseLayout>
